</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">While test-time scaling has revolutionized reasoning in large language models, generative video reasoning remains bottlenecked by a single-shot paradigm. We demonstrate that searching over denoising steps cannot rescue logically flawed rollouts because spatial trajectories commit early in the diffusion process. Root-level Best-of-<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (BoN) sampling is similarly inefficient: reasoning errors cluster early in the temporal axis, and resampling blindly discards verified upstream progress. To unlock effective test-time scaling for video models, we introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Temporal Backtracking Search (TBS)</span>, which shifts the search space to the temporal axis. TBS transforms video generation into an iterative generate–verify–restart loop via three core mechanisms: (1) <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">variable-<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> conditioning</span> to resume generation from arbitrary clean prefixes; (2) <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">temporal process verification</span> to localize failures and extract valid restart anchors; and (3) <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prefix-based search</span> to reallocate compute toward extending correct trajectories rather than root resampling. Across algorithmic, navigation, and robotics domains, TBS Pareto-dominates matched-budget BoN. In a strict out-of-distribution setting where one-shot generation collapses (<math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> for BoN), TBS achieves <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>22.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22.7\%</annotation></semantics></math>, with every solved episode stemming from a restarted branch. Ultimately, TBS reveals that the local reasoning competence of video models far exceeds what single-shot rollouts indicate, providing a scalable test-time framework to unlock it.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Generative video models are evolving beyond visual content creation to emerge as world simulators for sequential decision-making. By extracting action plans from generated rollouts, agents can search, plan, and solve complex tasks entirely in imagination <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. In this reasoning-oriented regime, the relevant axis for scaling test-time compute is no longer aesthetic quality but <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trajectory correctness</span>—whether the generated video depicts a logically valid solution to the given problem.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13861v1/main.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/124;" width="476" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Trajectories are determined by the earliest denoising steps<span id="S1.F1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. Decoding the same noisy latent at successive denoising steps shows that the motion plan is fixed within the first few steps and persists through to the final rollout. </span>Takeaway<span id="S1.F1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Additional compute spent on later denoising steps cannot redirect a wrong trajectory, so test-time search for video reasoning must branch over temporal frames, not over denoising steps.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While recent test-time scaling methods for video generation optimize over noise schedules or partially denoised latents to improve visual fidelity and prompt alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, they fall short for logical decision-making. Consequently, for temporal scaling, the community typically defaults to Best-of-<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> (BoN) sampling, treating an entire video rollout as an indivisible temporal block.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We identify two empirical bottlenecks that cause these paradigms to fail in long-horizon reasoning. First, decoding intermediate latents reveals that video models commit to a high-level spatial trajectory within the earliest denoising steps (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Temporal Backtracking Search for Test-time Generative Video Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>); allocating test-time compute to later denoising steps therefore cannot rescue a trajectory that is logically flawed from the start. Second, reasoning failures are not uniformly distributed along the trajectory; they cluster heavily in the early stages of generation. Root-level BoN thus wastes compute by repeatedly resampling the same early failure regions, discarding the valid partial prefixes generated upstream.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These observations motivate shifting the search space from the denoising axis to the <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">temporal</span> axis. Instead of discarding an entire rollout upon a single mistake, search algorithms should isolate the failure, salvage verified preceding frames, and reallocate compute to repair the invalid suffix. We introduce <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Temporal Backtracking Search (TBS)</span>, transforming single-shot video generation into an iterative generate–verify–restart loop. TBS realizes prefix-based temporal search via three core components: (1) <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">variable-<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> conditioning</span>, a curriculum-trained capability that enables the diffusion model to resume from arbitrary clean prefixes while preserving prior motion context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>; (2) <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">temporal process verification</span>, which identifies the first failure frame to extract verified restart anchors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>; and (3) <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prefix-based search</span>, which reallocates inference compute to extend valid trajectories rather than resampling from the root <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate TBS across three regimes spanning the realistic spectrum of process supervision: exact symbolic verification in algorithmic grid-worlds, learned noisy trajectory prediction in continuous navigation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, and simulator-replay verification in robotic manipulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Across all three domains, TBS Pareto-dominates matched-budget BoN. Notably, in a strict out-of-distribution (OOD) setting where one-shot generation collapses (<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> exact match for BoN), TBS achieves <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>22.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22.7\%</annotation></semantics></math>, with every solved episode stemming from a restarted child branch. Our primary contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Identifying two critical bottlenecks in video test-time scaling: early trajectory commitment renders denoising-step search ineffective, and clustered early failures make root-level BoN grossly inefficient.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proposing <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Temporal Backtracking Search (TBS)</span>, which transitions video test-time scaling from monolithic single-shot sampling to prefix-based temporal correction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Demonstrating empirically that temporal backtracking unlocks and stitches together the underlying local reasoning competence of video models under symbolic, learned, and physics-based verification.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test-time scaling and process verification.</span> Test-time scaling via Best-of-<math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and tree search has transformed LLM reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, driven by the insight that <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">process</em> verifiers scoring intermediate steps strictly outperform <em id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">outcome</em> verifiers scoring only final answers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. TBS adapts this paradigm to generative video reasoning by shifting evaluation from monolithic rollouts to verified prefixes. Crucially, our process verifier localizes <em id="S2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">where</em> a trajectory deviates, rather than merely judging <em id="S2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">whether</em> the terminal sequence is flawed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Long-horizon video generation.</span> Diffusion Forcing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> demonstrates causal continuation from clean prefixes as a native sequence diffusion capability, providing the modeling primitive for our variable-<math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> conditioning. While generative stitching methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> also compose long horizons from local segments, they heavily rely on bidirectional harmonization or global joint resampling. In contrast, TBS performs causal, verifier-localized repair, leaving verified pasts strictly untouched.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test-time scaling for video generation.</span> Recent methods scale inference compute for video diffusion by searching over denoising trajectories or partially denoised latents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. However, these approaches operate entirely on the <em id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">denoising</em> axis to optimize visual aesthetics and prompt alignment. TBS fundamentally differs by searching the <em id="S2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">temporal</em> axis to optimize trajectory correctness, leveraging process supervision that ranges from symbolic rules to simulator replay. Furthermore, while concurrent studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> diagnose the long-horizon reasoning limits of video models, they offer no constructive mitigation—a critical gap TBS bridges via prefix-based restartable generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.13861v1/method.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/132;" width="476" height="132"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Mechanism of Temporal Backtracking Search (TBS).<span id="S3.F2.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
(a) </span>Single-shot generation:<span id="S3.F2.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The trajectory is sampled in one pass; a single error at frame <math id="S3.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> invalidates the entire remaining suffix.
(b) </span>TBS:<span id="S3.F2.10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Decomposes long-horizon reasoning into short-horizon sub-problems by iteratively extending verified prefixes. The algorithm executes a generate–verify–restart loop: 1) </span>Generate<span id="S3.F2.10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> a candidate rollout; 2) </span>Verify<span id="S3.F2.10.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> the trajectory to localize the first failure and cache the valid prefix; and 3) </span>Restart<span id="S3.F2.10.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> generation from the highest-priority anchor.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To break the single-shot generative bottleneck, we reformulate video reasoning as inference-time search over the temporal axis. This section formalizes the <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">conditional reuse gap</span> that drives our approach (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Problem Formulation and the Conditional Reuse Gap ‣ 3 Method ‣ Temporal Backtracking Search for Test-time Generative Video Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), introduces the <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Temporal Backtracking Search (TBS)</span> algorithm designed to exploit this gap (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Temporal Backtracking Search (TBS) ‣ 3 Method ‣ Temporal Backtracking Search for Test-time Generative Video Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), and details the variable-<math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> generative engine required to enable temporal branching (Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Variable-𝐾 Conditioning ‣ 3 Method ‣ Temporal Backtracking Search for Test-time Generative Video Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Formulation and the Conditional Reuse Gap</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}=(v_{1},\ldots,v_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}=(v_{1},\ldots,v_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> be a video of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> frames generated from an initial image <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and a text prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. For sequential reasoning tasks, we define the logical correctness of a trajectory via a transition validity function:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi(v_{1},\ldots,v_{t})=\mathbb{I}\!\left[\,v_{i}\to v_{i+1}\text{ is valid }\forall\,i\leq t{-}1\,\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mi>𝕀</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">[</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mtext> is valid </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(v_{1},\ldots,v_{t})=\mathbb{I}\!\left[\,v_{i}\to v_{i+1}\text{ is valid }\forall\,i\leq t{-}1\,\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A video achieves an <em id="S3.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exact match</em> (EM <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=1</annotation></semantics></math>) if and only if <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\mathbf{v})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\mathbf{v})=1</annotation></semantics></math> and the trajectory reaches the goal state by frame <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. For invalid rollouts, we identify the earliest logical failure as the <em id="S3.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">first failure frame</em>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\mathbf{v})=\min\{t:\phi(v_{1},\ldots,v_{t})=0\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\mathbf{v})=\min\{t:\phi(v_{1},\ldots,v_{t})=0\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\mathbf{v})=T{+}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\mathbf{v})=T{+}1</annotation></semantics></math> if the video is fully correct. This failure index naturally partitions the rollout into a valid prefix <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1:F-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1:F-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and an invalid suffix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Root-level scaling paradigms, such as BoN, indiscriminately discard this verified prefix upon encountering a failure. To illustrate why this is suboptimal, consider a ground-truth trajectory <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> where a generated sample matches <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> up to frame <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. A root-level generator must synthesize the entire future trajectory from the initial frame, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{\mathrm{root}}=p_{\theta}(\mathbf{v}^{*}_{2:T}\mid v_{1}^{*},p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>root</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{root}}=p_{\theta}(\mathbf{v}^{*}_{2:T}\mid v_{1}^{*},p)</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas prefix repair only needs to resolve the remaining suffix, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{\mathrm{repair}}(\ell)=p_{\theta}(\mathbf{v}^{*}_{\ell+1:T}\mid\mathbf{v}^{*}_{1:\ell},p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>repair</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{repair}}(\ell)=p_{\theta}(\mathbf{v}^{*}_{\ell+1:T}\mid\mathbf{v}^{*}_{1:\ell},p)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We define the discrepancy in modeling difficulty between <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathrm{root}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>root</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{root}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathrm{repair}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>repair</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathrm{repair}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">conditional reuse gap</span>. Because reasoning errors accumulate multiplicatively over time, extending a clean partial sequence is inherently more tractable than generating a long-horizon rollout from scratch. TBS explicitly exploits this gap.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Temporal Backtracking Search (TBS)</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l0.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Initial frame </span><math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">v</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l0.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, prompt </span><math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l0.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, budgets </span><math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k_{1},s,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k_{1},s,m)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l0.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, Verifier </span><math id="alg1.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l0.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, Generator </span><math id="alg1.l0.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l0.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Initialize priority frontier </span><math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℱ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> {Pool of verified prefix anchors </span><math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(K,\mathbf{v}_{1:R},\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐯</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">π</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K,\mathbf{v}_{1:R},\pi)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Sample </span><math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> root rollouts from </span><math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> conditioned on </span><math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K{=}1,[v_{1}],p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">v</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K{=}1,[v_{1}],p)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
