<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 15: </span>Best EM per root width <math id="A2.T15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> at fixed budget <math id="A2.T15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B{=}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B{=}20</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="A2.T15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\text{domain},k_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>domain</mtext><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\text{domain},k_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> cell we take the maximum EM among all swept TBS configurations with that root width that fit the budget. Bold marks the best <math id="A2.T15.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> per domain. 2D Maze and Maze3D are essentially <math id="A2.T15.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-insensitive (range <math id="A2.T15.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> pp); Sokoban requires wider roots (<math id="A2.T15.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+6.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+6.2</annotation></semantics></math> pp from <math id="A2.T15.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.T15.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}{=}8</annotation></semantics></math>). Strict-OOD Keys-and-Doors prefers a balanced <math id="A2.T15.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}{=}4</annotation></semantics></math> versus BoN-20 at <math id="A2.T15.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.7</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SS10.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reading.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS10.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS10.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The root-width-sensitivity range across domains exposes the operating mode of TBS in each:</p>
<ul id="A2.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maze and Maze3D</span> are <math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-insensitive (range <math id="A2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> pp): TBS converges to nearly the same EM regardless of root width, and depth does the work.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sokoban</span> requires wider roots: a single-root TBS run (<math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}{=}2</annotation></semantics></math>) yields only <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="76.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>76.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">76.5\%</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}{=}8</annotation></semantics></math> reaches <math id="A2.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="82.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>82.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">82.7\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Sokoban prefixes can be visually plausible but already strategically dead, so additional independent roots are required to escape shallow-rollback traps.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sliding Puzzle and Keys-Doors ID</span> prefer slightly wider roots (<math id="A2.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}\in\{4,5,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}\in\{4,5,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> pp spread).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Strict-OOD Keys-and-Doors</span> prefers a balanced <math id="A2.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}{=}4</annotation></semantics></math>, but every <math id="A2.I3.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> value yields <math id="A2.I3.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 18.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>18.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 18.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> EM versus BoN-20’s <math id="A2.I3.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7\%</annotation></semantics></math>. The mechanism (prefix reuse), not the specific shape, is what matters.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS11" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.11 </span>Depth–Width Search Shape</h3>

<div id="A2.SS11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The matched-budget table above isolates the effect of <math id="A2.SS11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> at the single budget <math id="A2.SS11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B{=}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B{=}20</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we show the full root-width versus budget interaction. For each domain and each root width <math id="A2.SS11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}\in\{2,4,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}\in\{2,4,8\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we take the best EM achievable across all swept <math id="A2.SS11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,m)</annotation></semantics></math> configurations satisfying <math id="A2.SS11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}+s(m-1)=B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}+s(m-1)=B</annotation></semantics></math> at every available <math id="A2.SS11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Lines only span the budgets where that <math id="A2.SS11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> was actually swept.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.13861v1/root_budget_curves.svg" id="A2.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/253;" width="476" height="253"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A2.F8.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Per-domain root-width <math id="A2.F8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> versus generation budget <math id="A2.F8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Each panel shows the best TBS EM (%) at every swept <math id="A2.F8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k_{1},B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k_{1},B)</annotation></semantics></math> combination for that domain; dashed gray is the BoN-<math id="A2.F8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> reference for <math id="A2.F8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\{1,5,10,20\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\{1,5,10,20\}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="A2.F8.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sokoban</span> shows the strongest <math id="A2.F8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> separation (wider roots dominate at every budget). <span id="A2.F8.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2D Maze</span> prefers <math id="A2.F8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> (depth-friendly), with the narrow-and-deep curve sitting above wider variants at every budget. <span id="A2.F8.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3D Maze</span> and <span id="A2.F8.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keys-Doors ID</span> are nearly <math id="A2.F8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-insensitive at the saturation ceiling. Strict-OOD Keys-and-Doors is omitted from this plot because its absolute EM range (0–23%) cannot be meaningfully compared to in-distribution scales; per-budget numbers are reported in Table <a href="#A2.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ B.10 Root-Width Sensitivity at Matched Budget ‣ Appendix B Structured Video Reasoning Details ‣ Temporal Backtracking Search for Test-time Generative Video Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> and the strict-OOD path-cell breakdown in Table <a href="#A2.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ B.7.3 Keys-and-Doors strict-OOD path-cell breakdown ‣ B.7 Structured Video Reasoning Extended Results ‣ Appendix B Structured Video Reasoning Details ‣ Temporal Backtracking Search for Test-time Generative Video Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SS11.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross-domain takeaway.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS11.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The figure shows three regimes consistent with the per-domain analysis:</p>
<ul id="A2.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Depth-friendly (2D Maze):</span> <math id="A2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> dominates wider variants at every budget. Narrow-deep search converts each extra budget unit into prefix-reuse depth, which keeps producing new solves (cumulative EM by depth in Table <a href="#A2.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Cumulative EM by search depth (2D Maze). ‣ B.9 Root Solves vs. Restarted-Branch Solves ‣ Appendix B Structured Video Reasoning Details ‣ Temporal Backtracking Search for Test-time Generative Video Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> reaches <math id="A2.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> at depth 6).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Width-friendly (Sokoban):</span> <math id="A2.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}{=}8</annotation></semantics></math> dominates at every budget. Sokoban prefixes are strategically fragile, so wider roots are required to escape shallow-rollback traps before deeper search becomes useful.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Saturated (3D Maze, Keys-Doors ID):</span> all three TBS curves cluster within <math id="A2.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> pp of one another and well above the BoN reference. The mechanism helps but the operating ceiling limits visible separation across <math id="A2.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS12" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.12 </span>Verifier-Quality Ablations: Sokoban BFS-Aware and Sliding Undo-Required</h3>

<div id="A2.SS12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Sokoban and Sliding Puzzle headline TBS rows in the main paper use domain-specific verifier upgrades. Sokoban uses <span id="A2.SS12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SOKOBAN_VERIFIER_MODE=smart</span> with <span id="A2.SS12.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SCORE_MODE=progress</span>, which exposes a BFS dead-state and remaining-moves signal; without it, the verifier returns priority based purely on prefix length (the <em id="A2.SS12.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prefix-only</em> verifier). Sliding Puzzle uses <span id="A2.SS12.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SLIDING_SEMANTIC_POLICY=undo_required</span>, which marks any move whose only optimal next move is to undo the previous move as a <em id="A2.SS12.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semantic</em> failure and triggers rollback to the prior settled state; the alternative <span id="A2.SS12.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SLIDING_SEMANTIC_POLICY=none</span> waits for a syntactic transition error. This appendix isolates how much each verifier upgrade contributes to TBS, holding generation, dataset, and search structure fixed.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13861v1/appendix_figure/verifier_ablation_sokoban_sliding_panel.png" id="A2.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/190;" width="476" height="190" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F9.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A2.F9.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Verifier-quality ablations for state-aware restart.<span id="A2.F9.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> </span>(a)<span id="A2.F9.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> On Sokoban, the prefix-only verifier underperforms because long visual prefixes can already be strategically dead; adding BFS-based state verification and progress priority recovers the gap. </span>(b)<span id="A2.F9.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> On Sliding Puzzle, undo-required semantic rollback improves TBS across budgets while the corresponding BoN-20 reference does not improve, showing that the gain comes from cleaner restart anchors rather than a more permissive terminal evaluator.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SS12.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sokoban: state-aware verification is necessary.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sokoban is the clearest verifier-quality ablation in the structured suite because visual prefix length is not the same as useful progress. A rollout can be visually legal for many frames while pushing the box into a dead state from which the target is unreachable. Conditioning on the longest prefix then preserves the wrong strategic state, and TBS repeatedly re-attempts from a prefix that should have been rejected.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compare three variants on the same Sokoban. The <em id="A2.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prefix-only</em> variant ranks nodes by prefix length and does not use BFS dead-state information. The <em id="A2.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">BFS prefix-search</em> variant uses the BFS-aware Sokoban verifier to reject unsolvable box states, but still uses the standard prefix-based priority. The <em id="A2.SS12.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">progress-aware</em> variant is the headline setting: it ranks EM first, then BFS non-dead state, fewer BFS remaining moves, BFS progress, and finally the prefix fallback.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T16" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A2.T16.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A2.T16.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T16.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="A2.T16.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TBS config</span></th>
<th id="A2.T16.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="A2.T16.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Budget</span></th>
<th id="A2.T16.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="A2.T16.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prefix-only verifier</span></th>
<th id="A2.T16.3.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="A2.T16.3.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Progress-aware verifier</span></th>
<th id="A2.T16.3.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A2.T16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.T16.3.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> verifier</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.T16.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T16.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A2.T16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}{=}2,s{=}3,m{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}{=}2,s{=}3,m{=}3</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T16.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="A2.T16.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">8</span></td>
<td id="A2.T16.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A2.T16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="399/600=66.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">399</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">600</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">66.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">399/600=66.5\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T16.3.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A2.T16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="424/600=70.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">424</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">600</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">70.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">424/600=70.7\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T16.3.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A2.T16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+4.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">4.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+4.2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.T16.3.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> pp</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T16.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T16.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A2.T16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}{=}2,s{=}2,m{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}{=}2,s{=}2,m{=}5</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A2.T16.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="A2.T16.3.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">10</span></td>
<td id="A2.T16.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A2.T16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="401/600=66.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">401</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">600</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">66.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">401/600=66.8\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T16.3.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="A2.T16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="436/600=72.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">436</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">600</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">72.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">436/600=72.7\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
